@tadityasrinivas Point is: post training is so insanely expensive because RL is so damn sample inefficient. And much of it is not really RL anyway.

In the real world, which is what my slide discussed, RL can only be a minor contributor because of the sample inefficiency.

And no, the systems that prove theorems aren't auto-regressive.

@ranig @elonmusk @Liv_Boeree Haha. Don't hold your breath until humanoids can clean your house. 

Also, LLMs still can't do arithmetics. They use tools for that (and for many other things). Like humans. 
AI systems that do math produce outputs by search/trial and error, which is precisely what I was advocating (rather than auto-regressive token prediction, which is what garden-variety LLMs do).

@phillip_isola @john__allard Clearly, errors are not independent. I do state that it is a hypothesis in my model,
But no, errors cannot tae you back into the correct set. It's a tree of token sequences with terminal nodes being <end of sequence> tags. There is no possible coming back: it's a tree!

Things are different in continuous space, I agree.

